    [quoting me:]
>   Our responsibility is only to ourselves, to not fuck up the ecology such
>   that it can no longer sustain us.  Since beavers (e.g.) might perform some
>   important function in the ability of the Earth to support human life, we
>   probably shouldn't wipe them all out until we know for sure that they
>   don't.  Pretending that we owe them something, that they are agents and
>   have some kind of rights just because they exist, is silly.  (NOTE:  I am
>   not a "natural law" believer; I base my moral system on aesthetics.  I
>   don't believe that humans have any "rights" either.  They do, however, have
>   the capability to enter into contracts with me giving us each "rights" with
>   respect to each other, and so I am willing to do that to our mutual
>   benefit.)

I have taken the liberty of rearranging the order of what Andy says, in
order to address his points in logical sequence.

>I think you are rationalizing your
>system by looking for a convenient way to separate humans from animals.

I don't attempt to "rationalize" my system to anyone.  All ethical systems
have the same basis, the arational aesthetic prejudices of their adherents.
 I just admit it.  Theists and Platonists muddy the waters with spooks like
"God" and "natural law", but they're just _rationalizing_ their own
preferences.

>The only thing you can really say is that you think humans have rights
>because you have decided that humans should, since you are a human, and 
>animals shouldn't, according to your personal preference.  Any attempt 
>at rationalizing this is bound to be full of holes.

I'm sorry; I thought that was what I _did_ say.  If you were merely
attempting to restate my position, you have done so correctly.  I sure
didn't attempt to rationalize it.

>Philosophically,  this is not much different than saying that only white
>men,  or only white people,  or only left-handed people,  etc.,  should
>have rights.  The category of who has rights is arbitrary,  but the
>real argument is the same:  I belong to group X,  and I don't care about
>anyone who is not in group X,  therefore only group X has rights.

Right.  What defines the line around Group X is your definition of "people"
(which may differ from your definition of "humans").  Primitive tribes
define "people" as "my tribe"; all other humans are non-people, barbarians.
 More modern tribes define "people" as "all humans", recognizing that
humans different from "us" in skin color or beliefs are "people" too.  Such
definitions are ARBITRARY, or derived from arbitrary postulates.

You're also employing the rhetorical fallacy of bifurcation if you lump
everything outside of that line into one category.  Group X might have one
set of rights; Group Y might have a different set of rights; Group Z might
have no rights.  Every organism might have a different set of rights.  You
can slice the pie any number of ways.

I want to state clearly that just being rational doesn't confer any
"rights", either.  "Group X" as defined is the group I consider "people",
but until we've entered into some kind of communication I don't have any
obligations to them; I'm just being nice, in the hopes that they'll be nice
too.

>What about humans who, through mental disability or extreme youth, are not
>able to understand the concept of a contract?  Would they be in the same
>category as cattle in your ethics?

No, but they're not "people" either.  (What is it with this bifurcation
kick?  The Universe can count higher than two!)  It would be aesthetically
displeasing to me to kill or torture them or to witness them being killed
or tortured, but their opinions don't count for anything.  If I owned a
grassy vacant lot where children or morons sometimes came to play, and
decided I wanted to build a house on that site, I wouldn't care how the
children or morons felt about it.

>Let me make it clear that I am not accusing you of anything,  I'm just
>questioning your rationalization of your system of ethics.

Question no longer:  I don't believe that any rationalization of any system
of ethics is possible.  People want what they want, arationally, and they
reify "gods" and "inner essences" to try to justify wanting what they want,
but the fact is, they just want it.

    [quoting me again, talking to Jim somebody:]
>   You, on the other hand, seem to indicate that other organisms have "rights"
>   independent of their utility for humans.  I find that interesting and would
>   like to know why you think this (if in fact you do).
>
>I'll use your criterion of "contracts".

Go get your own criterion; this one's mine! :-)

Here's why contracts enter into it.  I like cooperation and I dislike
violence.  If I can reason with an entity, we can agree not to initiate
violence against each other (and we can agree on a definition of violence),
and we can establish penalties should one of us break the contract and
initiate violence.  Now, if he possesses firepower superior to mine, I may
not be able to enforce the contract if he violates it, but if there are a
whole bunch of us, and we agree that we will all act to ensure enforcement
of the contract if one of us starts to inflict violence on one of the
others, then the combined firepower of all the signatories (or their
authorized agent) is theoretically greater than the individual firepower of
this lone rogue, and, reasonably assured that we've cleared up the violence
thing, we can all turn our attention to more productive matters.  We can
make other agreements contingent on non-violence, and we can even contract
for mutual defense from outside attack as well.

Now... I can't enter into a contract with a rattlesnake (or a hippo or a
vole or a sea anemone, but let's stick with the rattlesnake).  Rattlesnakes
aren't rational, and even though our protection agency presents a credible
threat, a rattlesnake isn't going to hesitate to bite me.  I have no
"rights" in his perception; he's not capable of recognizing any "rights" of
mine, either "natural" ones or ones granted by contract -- I'm just a big
warm moving object in his field of vision.  This puts him fundamentally
outside any peaceful settlement of differences, and if he tries to bite me,
I'll defend myself with deadly force.  If he's in my back yard, and I
consider him a danger to my loved ones, I'll initiate deadly force against
him first because I have no reason to believe that he will hesistate to
initiate deadly force against them at some future time.  He can't be
reasoned with, so any steps I take in pre-emptive self-defense are
aesthetically OK to me.  I may just find him aesthetically displeasing
(suppose it's a termite colony instead of a rattlesnake).  I might be more
inclined to kill a rattlesnake and less inclined to kill a family of bunny
rabbits.

If the snake belongs to another "person" (as a pet or livestock), then I
may or may not be justified (according to our explicit agreement) in
killing his snake if it was on my property without permission.  He might
owe me a fine for letting his snake run around loose.  This lies outside
the area of my personal aesthetics, and into rights I have specifically
granted by contract.

Back to the general case:  I choose to grant rights to rational beings in
exchange for their granting rights in return to me.  Irrational beings
can't grant me any rights in any meaningful way that I can be assured they
will adhere to in future.  Therefore they fall into non-people categories
(property, beneficial wildlife, pests), each of which is handled
differently from the others in ways defined by "people" (rational beings),
not by the members of the category.

>Domesticated animals (pets) generally have a relationship of trust with 
>their human caretakers.  Is that unlike a contract?

If people could enter into contracts with their household pets, there would
be no need for housebreaking... your dog would just agree not to pee on the
rug, and would hold to the agreement or face the penalties.  Since it
appears that dogs (1) can't abstract the concepts of future time and
restitution and (2) can't use much language, they are clearly not "people"
and the pet-human relationship is not a contract.

>There are a number of species close enough to humans in intelligence that
>they could probably understand the notion of a contract,  if we could
>communicate it to them.  Why exclude them?

"You should eat dolphin-safe tuna because dolphins are intelligent."
"If they're so damn intelligent, why can't they stay the hell out of the
tuna nets?"

But seriously, I would have no problem with granting "people" status to
such creatures.  It's just that it takes very sophisticated use of language
to understand the concept of a contract -- human children don't learn it
until about, what seven or eight years of age (if they learn it at all)? 
[How about it, Extroparents?  Is that about the right age?]  Last night on
one of those Discovery-type programs, my friends and I saw a parrot that
apparently understands what it is saying.  Researcher:  "What color is
this?"  Parrot: "Blue."  Researcher (holding out a tray of objects):  "Pick
the one that's red and has four corners."  (Parrot picks up red square.) 
This and the famous apes who use sign language are the most sophisticated
non-human language users I've heard of, but they don't seem to be
sufficiently capable of handling abstractions to say something like "I know
you've got some TV reporters coming by today.  I'll put on a good show for
them if you give me a toy."
