Date: Fri, 3 Sep 93 21:09:46 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Green Goo

Tony Hamilton:
> Nick wrote me in email, wondering how I could imagine an ever-increasing
> population on Earth...

If you are going to divulge private correspondence to the list at
least get it right.  I questioned your argument dismissing Jay Freeman with 
the claim that ever-increasing population, on or off Earth is practically 
inevetable, so why fight it.  I suggested you study the relevent
demographic literature.  I very much wish you were right, but the 
preponderance of evidence indicates that decreasing population and 
eventual genocide, both voluntary and coercive, are far more probable, 

Two major reasons, neither of them having to do with the
"limited resources" argument well debunked by Julian Simon et. al:

(1) The self-genociding space colony problem.  Cross-cultural
demographics show educated humans populations enter a long-term decline
that might end in complete genocide.  Some might go posthuman, a speculative 
outcome which opens up the question on a much wider front -- why would 
a piece of software have strong extropian motivations, either to make 
copies or for internal growth?  The notion that merely colonizing new 
territory increases birthrate is a hugely popular, very wrong piece 
of wishful thinking which ignores  not only demographics facts (eg
birth rate far higher in Bangladesh than Australia, and hundreds
of other examples), but the fundamental changes brought about by 
effective birth control in the 20th century which separates sexual 
motivation from reproductive success.  The structure that successfully 
emerged is now irrevocably broken. The self-genociding space colony was 
a big thread on this list last year which I don't care to rehash -- 
those want to argue the point, I hope will first study the relevant 
demographic literature, eg fertility rate vs. education
level across cultures, and not simply rehash the popular but
wrong wishful thinking that every space colonization booster, including
me, would like to believe on this issue.

(2) The Green Goo problem: quite similar to (1), except it 
involves coercive use of released viruses instead of 
voluntary self-genocide.  

In both cases we have obsoleted the fundamental extropian motivations that 
cause the emergence and growth of civilization, the biological instincts
that we like to crow about and assume are automatic, even
as we alter them oblivious to Extropian values.  But the desire for
growth is not at all inevitable.  Whatever is written in the genes
can be written around by culture, and there is no guaruntee
that culture will write the script for its further growth.
Long before technology has enabled a posthuman state, it has 
destroyed most important motivations for growth and perpetuation
that derive from human instinct.  As Extropians, we have
the rather lonely job of trying to stem the tide, at least
in our own little corner of the world.  That is, we have the
job if even we friends of civilization are sufficiently motivated
and capable of doing something, at least defending ourselves.

I'm a big believer in civilization, often (perhaps foolishly) at
the expense of myself.  I strongly believe much of what Heinlein
had to say, and what Julian Simon says in the current Extropy,
and with what the Extropian Values imply about these things.
Unfortuneately, my beliefs clash ever more
strongly with biological motivations (both mine, which I try to
fight when they conflict, and other's, which they typically don't) 
as we get more and more effective technologies on this planet 
applied towards assuaging obsolete biological motivations rather
than rational Extropian motivations.

Nukes and gas chambers are obvious threats that get our rapt 
biological attention.  Birth control and Green Goo do not; in the former 
case we are actively motivated to use it on ourselves.  And even flame
those who point out its negative side.  And to those even now
raising their flamethrowers my way, let me point out that I
am not advocating any sort of coercion to put the genie back in
the bottle, simply pointing out its major negative impacts we need
as individuals and Extropian nexi need to adapt to, but that most
people prefer to ignore, or deny the existence or importance of
the phenomenon, or to blindly fight as a violation of some abstract
supernatural code rather than as a shortsighted severing of the
threads which tie us to the future.

At this point I'm extremely pessimistic about even convincing
very many people that Green Goo is a threat, much less motivating
a defense against it, despite the overwhelming evidence to the rational 
mind, because of the lack of trigger to the emotional mind.  So at 
this point I'm pretty much out to save my own little corner of the 
world, or before even that to find a little corner world 
that is motivated to continue to live and grow through the Green Goo 
era.

> My question is, how would Green Goo be unlike the nuclear threat? 

A: Population control viruses can be created, from widely available 
technology, by a small group of people on a low budget.  Nuclear 
weapons require $10's of billions and thousands of highly skilled 
technicians to even start playing the game (the main bottleneck being 
uranium enrichment, which requires large, obvious factories).  Have
you ever heard of the "Cooperative Research Centre for Biological
Control of Vertebrate Pest Populations", a Australian government/
university research consortium made up of politicians, ecologists
and biotech engineers?  Neither had I until a read an obscure little
article[1].  There might be dozens of such labs around the planet,
with hundreds of highly motivated anthrophobes and biotech
engineers working together, far beyond the realm of extropian
cognizance or concern or contemplation of the consequences.
Their task is vastly easier than the corresponding task of finding
the virus once it gets out, designing a vaccine (takes from years
to decades), or finding a cure (never been done for a virus, since
they embed themselves in our very genetic code)[2].  The personal
steps needed to defend oneself against Green Goo vary widely depending
on the virus, and often open up a huge can of social worms
that require huge doses of both detatched rational thought and
strong personal motivation to overcome.

B: its effect is subtle, and even desirable for many people.  Use
of nukes has and will cause widespread motivation to protect oneself
(bomb shelters, SDI, etc.), but there is nothing to cause elemental
biological fear in Green Goo, any more than there is for birth
control. In fact, we've already seen people, even here on an Extropian
list, who are sympathetic to Green Goo, and I can assure you that there
are many highly educated people worldwide who would be sympathetic;
just check out sci.bio.ecology for a vicious sample.

C: Green Goo can completely eliminate our species and forever 
destroy civilization.  In other scenarios where it falls short
of that, it can reduce civilization to the point of destroying cryonics
and with it all hopes of reanimation, uploading, space colonization, 
etc.  And at the same time destroying any hope of more traditional
immortality through having and raising children.  Even full-scale 
nuclear war at the height of the Cold War would have been hard-pressed 
wipe out civilization, and the more probable terroristic uses of nukes 
on a city here, a city there wouldn't compromise cryonics, excepting 
unlucky coincidence of target and storage facility.

D: Green Goo is a much more subtle, complex problem against which
the most economical defenses are not at all obvious.
Green Goo bypasses our basic biologically motivated defenses in ways
that violent coercion never could.  We badly need cultural defenses, 
but they seem to be in very short supply -- both due to lack of 
motivation to find said defenses, and the complex, subtle, emotional,
and social nature of the extreme steps needed to defend against these 
plagues.

> if you're just going to take the stance that Green Goo will happen, and that
> there will be no possible reaction,

I'm not into these kind of absolute predictions or fixed religious
views of the future.  I'm just pointing out that its occurance
is much more probable, and is vastly more difficult and culturally 
wrenching to defend against, than you make it out to be.   So culturally
wrenching, that I suspect it will be a very hard task even convincing
the friends of civilization of its magnitude.  (Forget about its 
enemies; many of them will look on Green Goo sympathetically).
Even though the scientific facts are out there for anybody
to learn about, if they are both motivated and dispassionate enough.
Ah, there's the rub.  All humans, even we Extropians are dominated by 
our obsolete biology.

[1] Science v261:683 (6 Aug 93)
[2] Moore, _Lifespan: Who Lives Longer and Why_ describes how infectious
disease still ranks right up there with years of life lost in
developed countries, and far outstrips other causes of life lost
in underdeveloped countries, and describes how primitive our
medical state of art is in both finding diseases in the first place
and then preventing or curing them once found.

